On 1/31/2014 3:33 PM, Andrew Gelman wrote:
> HI, my quick hack would be to take the logit of the variable after transforming to a 0.5 to 4.5 scale, that is, work with logit((y - 0.5)/4).  This will make it continuous and will spread out the extremes a bit.  Of course it won't give correct predictions but otherwise it could do what you need.
> If it works (or if it doesn't), please let me know!
> A

I get this error:

  Error in stanc(file = file, model_code = model_code, model_name = model_name,  :
    failed to parse Stan model 'stan_model' with error message:
  EXPECTATION FAILURE LOCATION: file=input; line=14, column=21

     z <- logit((y-0.5)/4);
                      ^-- here

  DIAGNOSTIC(S) FROM PARSER:
  no matches for function name="subtract"
      arg 0 type=real[1]
      arg 1 type=real
  available function signatures for subtract:
  0.  subtract(vector, vector) : vector
  1.  subtract(row vector, row vector) : row vector
  2.  subtract(matrix, matrix) : matrix
  3.  subtract(vector, real) : vector
  4.  subtract(row vector, real) : row vector
  5.  subtract(matrix, real) : matrix
  6.  subtract(real, vector) : vector
  7.  subtract(real, row vector) : row vector
  8.  subtract(real, matrix) : matrix
  expression is ill formed
  Parser expecting: <eps>

The relevant block is:
transformed data {
    real z[N];
    z <- logit((y-0.5)/4);
}

The error seems to indicate stan can't subtract a constant from a
vector, but that seems unlikely.

Ross

P.S. I've been running a truncated normal model, but it had poor n_eff
(3 for 200 iterations) in tests and is taking a long time in a
production run.

> On Jan 31, 2014, at 11:49 PM, Ross Boylan wrote:
>
>> I have a variable that takes on values in the range 1-4.  I was thinking of modeling this as a beta distribution, but since there are a lot of cases at the extremes that seems problematic.  I'm not sure the beta function in stan will even accept such inputs and produce estimates.  I could fake it by pulling the values in a bit, but I'm not sure that's a good idea.
>>
>> I'd appreciate any suggestions on how to fit such outcomes.  I don't think it's a big deal if the fit doesn't reproduce the detailed features of the distribution for my purposes, though if this lack of fit leads to estimation problems that would matter.
>>
>> "Detailed" features refers to that fact that most of the observations are exactly integers, and all the values are evenly divisible by 1/3.
>>
>> My main focus is on how covariates shift the distribution.  One approach might be to treat the outcome as categorical or ordinal, but that seems likely to create a lot more parameters.
>>
>> The distribution also kind of looks like a truncated normal if one uses 1 unit size bins.
>>
>> If you're curious, here's the basic data:
>>
>>> table(tfat$FrndPartRace)
>>                1 1.33333333333333 1.66666666666667                2
