On 2/12/14, 8:56 PM, Bill Gillespie wrote:
> Hi Bob,
>
> We (a group of pharmacometricians at Metrum Research Group) have been exploring the use of stan as an alternative to
> other tools (BUGS, JAGS, NONMEM, ..). We are particularly interested in the proposed addition of ODE solvers to stan and
> their use for pharmacokinetic/pharmacodynamic (PKPD) data analysis. Here are a few comments on the “ODE Integrator
> Support” page on the stan wiki (https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/wiki/ODE-Integrator-Support). This input is informed in
> part by my experience developing the bugsModelLibrary add-on for WinBUGS.

Thanks!  We really appreciate insights from people who've tackled
these problems before.

> “The only robust solution to dosing is to integrate between dosings. This would, of course, require a more complicated
> input, with dose times and a delta for the state for each dose time.”
>
>     We concur with this approach to dealing with dosing events, but we would prefer to extend the approach to all
>     inter-event intervals where “event” refers to dosing and observation events. Intermediate user-specified events
>     might also be included. This permits parameters that vary with time as a piecewise constant function. This is the

That makes sense and is actually easier for us to implement.

What's an intermediate user-specified event?  We've gotten requests
to allow arbitrary tweaks to compartment concentration --- can the
be formulated that way?

I think the motivation for how we're doing it now was efficiency
of the integrator and the limited problems we're currently looking at.

>     approach used in NONMEM’s PREDPP library and bugsModelLibrary. NONMEM and bugsModelLibrary also provide good
>     examples for how you might structure the arguments for stan function calls. The approach is flexible and has the
>     benefit of already being familiar to a large fraction of pharmacometricians who might wish to use stan for
>     pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamic modeling.

I find NONMEM really impenetrable, but Daniel and I have made
some headway in understanding it.  I'll try looking into
bugsModelLibrary, which I got another pointer to, so I dropped
it into the Wiki:

   https://code.google.com/p/bugsmodellibrary/

> “Dosing Convenience Methods: steady-state dosing (i.e. dosing at a fixed time interval) and multiple dosing
> specifications (i.e. one only states “I want 50 dosing events with a between-dose time of 8h and a certain amount”).
> Both situations frequently arise in PK modeling and can be handled analytically very efficiently.”
>
>     Non-steady-state multiple dosing of equal doses at equal time intervals is certainly a simple book-keeping matter of
>     introducing a sequence of new dosing events. The steady-state case is another story. You could use brute force,
>     i.e., simulate a large number of doses until apparent steady-state is achieved. A more efficient approach (usually)
>     is to treat this as a boundary value ODE problem. In other words use a numerical root solver to solve for the
>     initial conditions that result in steady-state.

I'm mostly concentrating on the design of the language.
I'll have to get help on the diff eqs for this one.  I'm not
very good at calc.

One difficulty for us is that we have to calculate derivatives of
all of our solutions w.r.t. parameters.   I think this is possible
with solvers.

> Finally we encourage you to implement the ODE support in stages. Stage 1 would implement a simple ODE solver.

That's a very good idea.  We'll probably restrict to the non-stiff
systems, too, to start, as it'll cut down on the burden for derivatives.

> Support
> for dosing and other events could be implemented in subsequent stages. This is because the stan language is sufficiently
> flexible that the user can write stan code that implements dosing and observation (and other) events and call the ODE
> solver for each inter-event interval. Thus having a simple ODE solver is sufficient for most PKPD modeling problems
> albeit not as convenient or efficient as having the event schedule handled by built-in code.

We're doing more or less this for some applications now.

> The attached zip file contains R and stan code that illustrates user-written code for such recursive calculations. In
> this case it uses the analytical solution for a linear 2 compartment model with 1st order absorption, but you can
> imagine that a call to a numerical ODE solver could replace the analytic calculations.

Thanks again.  I'll take a look.

> Thanks for all the stan team's efforts in developing such a flexible analysis platform.

You're welcome.  We're having a blast building Stan and
are overwhelmed by the amount of feedback we've gotten.

- Bob

