>>      > If you supply less water than gamma, your plants die and you
>> get no rice. If you supply more than gamma, then the
>>     rice
>>      > harvest increases with water, asymptotically approaching alpha
>> when the water is much greater than gamma. We are
>>      > studying drought and water scarcity, so in many years water is
>> close enough to gamma that we can't ignore it.
>>      >
>>      > The goal of the analysis is to find alpha, beta, and gamma for
>> each village, with estimates of their uncertainty.
>>     These
>>      > numbers will then be used in an econometric analysis of the
>> marginal value of changing the supply of water for
>>     irrigation.
>>      >
>>      > I'm not sure yet how I want to model the noise (the stochastic
>> variation in rice from year to year if water is held
>>      > constant). The amount of rice has to be greater than or equal
>> to zero, so I could add noise drawn from a
>>     distribution
>>      > before applying the max(0, ...) function but maybe there's a
>> more efficient way to do it.
>>
>>      > My student fit the parameters alpha, beta, and gamma for each
>> village individually, using a canned gradient search
>>      > function in Matlab, but some of the villages have short
>> records and never get enough water to clearly observe the
>>      > asymptote at alpha, so the fits are not well constrained.
>> After reading Phil's post at Andrew's blog "Everything
>>     I need
>>      > to know about Bayesian statistics, I learned in eight schools
>>      >
>> <http://andrewgelman.com/2014/01/21/everything-need-know-bayesian-statistics-learned-eight-schools/
>>
>> <http://andrewgelman.com/2014/01/21/everything-need-know-bayesian-statistics-learned-eight-schools/>>"
>> I'm thinking
>>     that
>>      > this is a lot like his radon in Minnesota problem and it would
>> be better to model the data together, using a
>>      > hierarchical treatment of inter-village variation in alpha,
>> beta, and gamma by drawing each village's parameters
>>     from a
>>      > parent distribution, so the villages with lots of data and
>> lots of variation in water can help constrain alpha on
>>     the
>>      > villages with short records and/or little variation in water.
>>      >
>>      > However, I am new to MCMC analysis and have some trepidation
>> at diving into Stan with a model that's both
>>     nonlinear and
>>      > not continuously differentiable (as the note at the beginning
>> of section 29.5 in the manual warns). Is this
>>     problem just
>>      > too pathological, or is it reasonable to try Stan?
>>
>>     You might run into the same problem trying to calculate an MLE,
>>     because the terms drop out of the likelihood, too.
>>
>>      > I am working on coding a model using rstan and generating
>> synthetic data with known alpha, beta, and gamma to see
>>      > whether I can recover the parameters, but I'm new to this and
>> slow, so I figured that I would ask the experts for
