Dear Stan-folks,

I've been tinkering at some code for a non-linear regression model (which I have posted about before <https://groups.google.com/d/topic/stan-users/GBLbw8dftec/discussion>), and have gotten stuck with horribly mixing chains. This problem has appeared since I upgraded to rstan 2.1.0 and 2.2.0.

Sometime in August 2013, I got the model running successfully and managed to fit it to a dataset with the latest version of rstan available then (can't tell the version from what I've saved). Back then, not all distributions had yet been vectorized, so upon the release of rstan 2.1.0 I thought I might try and vectorize everything and see if things would speed up. Coding up the vectorization was no problem, but as it turned out, since rstan 2.1.0 (I've tried 2.2.0 as well) the sampler simply does not seem to converge to the right mode anymore, churns out a handfull of effective sample sizes (N < 20) no matter how much brute force applied, and is   v e r y   slow at sampling. When sequentially sampling multiple chains, upon completion of the first chain, R sometimes even freezes completely (even with as few as 1K iterations). Reverting back to the unvectorized code did not change these findings. I have also tried implementing the "Matt trick" for the log-normally distributed random effects (defining unbounded raw random effects, sampling these from standard normal distribution, and transforming these in the transformed parameters block into lognormally distributed random effects - no correction for posterior curvature required, right?) - the sampler either slowed down to a grinding halt, or spitted out an endless stream of red Metropolis rejections.

Anyhow, to start afresh, I've coded up a script that simulates data such as the model would predict it, and I have tried to fit the model to these simulated data. The result remains horrible mixing. Any advice that you may have would be highly appreciated. I'm about to bash my head into a wall. For your convenience, I've attached:
Script to simulate data and fit the completely vectorized model to simulated data. The choice of using full mass matrix was based on my earlier impression that it really helped effective sampling back in August 2013. Mind the potential warning in the middle of the script about censored observations after first positive observations.
PDF with fit of posterior to subset of real data, August 2013 (some version of rstan before 2.0.0); to give you some idea of what things should be looking like. :) [Edit: the dashed line is the detection limit for the response; observations under the detection limit are censored in the model, i.e. integrated out using a cumulative log-normal distribution).]

And via google drive:
PDF <https://drive.google.com/file/d/0BxTB9IYjuodjQ1BfV2Rzb0huSDA/edit?usp=sharing> with visualization of the posteriors based on the real data, February 2014 (with rstan 2.2.0, viz produced with package ggmcmc). :(
PDF <https://drive.google.com/file/d/0BxTB9IYjuodjejh6LWxhc3FTVjg/edit?usp=sharing> with visualization of the posteriors based on the real data, August 2013 (some version of rstan before 2.0.0, viz produced with package ggmcmc). :)

Big fat thank you in advance to anyone who wants to chip in.

