Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/HUshsYBEtwA/m/PRnKBuvw3KQJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The code was largely refactored as of 2.x, but the goal was to maintain
the same sampling strategy.  Maybe Michael will have some more suggestions.

The only major difference you should find in 2.x versions (not
counting the buggy 2.0.y versions) are the larger acceptance
rate target (2.1.0) and initial step-size tuning based on the
actual initialization point rather than 0.

I don't know how much the full mass matrix estimation changed in
2.x.  But you might try turning that off.

In the print() of the results, what is the tree depth and acceptance
rate you're getting?

How are you initializing?  I would try init=0 or some value
of init=z where 0 < z << 2, say z = 0.25.   Or even better,
start at some realistic values, perhaps with some noise.

The other thing you can do is try turning the accept rate
target (delta) back down to the old default, 0.65.  A larger
target should mean more precise sampling, so an issue might
be that the new version of Stan is actually doing a better job
of sampling --- that is, you might actually have the kind of
multimodal posteriors it looks like you're getting.

Mac OS X Darwin (10.7) is pretty far behind in C++ compilers,
but I don't think that'd be an issue if you managed to compile
and do so with optimization (set_cppo("fast") was set in previous
version before you compiled next version and left on after compile).

The model itself is too complicated to comprehend.  The main thing
you have to do is make sure the model has support over any variables
that meet all the declared constraints.

The CDFs are slow, but they should have always been slow.

- Bob

