Hi Bob, Michael,

To answer Bob's questions:
 The PDFs on gDrive are from multiple chains, so yes, the chains aren't sampling from the same locations.
The earlier version of stan did not explore the region that the current sampler is exploring. This doesn't bode well for the previous estimates, although they made much more sense (biologically) than the current ones. But I'm probably biased on that part, wanting to see those nice catterpillar plots again.
Regarding the mass around the modes, I find it difficult to judge (or I am not getting the question right); in general the probability mass is distributed very irregularly in the lastest version of stan.
Good to hear you confirm that the parameter support is probably not the problem (although I don't understand how you can tell from the treedepth, this is beyond my grasp, although I'd love to understand it).
The quoted doc on initializing from R is exactly the one I'm using, so I'm not quite sure what I'm doing wrong. My init-generating function (see previous mail) basicly has the format that you describe ( init_fun <- function(x) { return(init_vals); } ). Just to check, I don't have to initialize the transformed parameters, right?

In response to Michaels' q's:
I can get the step sizes later today when I'm home. Is mean stepsize useful information, or would you like something more detailed? I am sorry but I don't know how to extract the mass matrix from a stan object in R. Note on the side: it would be helpful if the R help-file on stan and/or the manual would list what kind of values are in an object resulting from stan(fit = ..., data = ..., ...) in R, and how this information can be pulled.

Thanks for thinking with me!

Luc

