On 2/18/14, 12:48 AM, Luc Coffeng wrote:
> Hi Bob, Michael,
>
> To answer Bob's questions:
>

>   *   The PDFs on gDrive are from multiple chains, so yes, the chains aren't sampling from the same locations.

So that does seem to indicate that the chains are getting stuck in local modes.

>   * The earlier version of stan did not explore the region that the current sampler is exploring. This doesn't bode well

>     for the previous estimates, although they made much more sense (biologically) than the current ones. But I'm
>     probably biased on that part, wanting to see those nice catterpillar plots again.

This is probably where you want to focus debugging.

>   * Regarding the mass around the modes, I find it difficult to judge (or I am not getting the question right); in

>     general the probability mass is distributed very irregularly in the lastest version of stan.

Look at the points around the modes and see what their density is and how far out they
are. Hard to judge, but you can eyeball it.  Very coarsely, you can look at a histogram
of lp__ on each chain.  If one's very small compared to the others, you are probably stuck
in a bad local location.

Also look at step sizes.  One way to get stuck is to adapt to a very
small step size and then continually hit the tree depth boundary.

>   * Good to hear you confirm that the parameter support is probably not the problem (although I don't understand how you

>     can tell from the treedepth, this is beyond my grasp, although I'd love to understand it).

When models have parameters that go out of support, it usually shows
up early and often with rejections.  When NUTS wanders out of support,
it stops moving.  Tree depth = log2(number of steps moved), so when
tree depth is large, you moved many steps.  Now it could be that you moved
many steps because of very small step sizes.

>   * The quoted doc on initializing from R is exactly the one I'm using, so I'm not quite sure what I'm doing wrong. My
>     init-generating function (see previous mail) basicly has the format that you describe ( /init_fun <- function(x) {
>     return(init_vals); }/ ). Just to check, I don't have to initialize the transformed parameters, right?

