Just tested the model again with simulated data, using double the number of data points per individual, lower measurement error, and super-constrained parameters, practically telling the model what the values of the hyperparameters are. Oddly enough, the model severely underestimates all log-normally distributed child-parameters (t[], m[], and r[]) relative to the estimated mean of their parent-hyperparameter (T1, M1, and R). Even more oddly enough, the parent-hyperparameter for the means themselves do not seem to be pulled towards the lower bound of their support, and the standard deviations S_T1, S_M1, and S_R are not pushed towards their upper bound to allow the crazy values of their child-parameters. This discrepancy is most obvious for parameter M1 and its children m[]. See excerpt from output below and attached pdf with density plots. Could it be that something is off with the log-normal density in rstan 2.2.0?

