Thanks for your reply Bob. It is the mixture modeling section.
For my data set, I have individuals coming from different units, so a simple two-level structure. Three responses were modeled via a factor model. The factor loadings depend on both fixed (individual-level covariate x1) and random effects (u.l). I put part of my codes here:

for (i in 1:N)
# N is the total number of individuals
{
...
(lamx1[B[unit[i]],1:3]*x1[i]+u.l[unit[i],1:3])*F[i]
# This is the factor part. x1 is individual-level covariate

F[i]~dnorm(0,1)
# factor follows a standard normal distribution
}

for (i in 1:nu)
# nu is the total number of units
{
...
B0[i]~dbern(0.5)
B[i]<-B0[i]+1
# B is the indicator for mixture prior of u.l

u.l[i,1:3]~dmnorm(lamx0[B[i],1:3],tau.ul[,])
# a mixture of two normal distributions having the same variance
...
}

for (i in 1:3)
{
lamx0[1,i]<-lam0[i]
lam0[i]~dnorm(0,0.000001)
lamx0[2,i]<-lam0[i]*(-1)

lamx1[1,i]<-lam1[i]
lam1[i]~dnorm(0,0.000001)
lamx1[2,i]<-lam1[i]*(-1)
...
}

I hope it is clear for you to understand my question.
This model was actually published online a few months ago: http://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/sim.6062/abstract. It would be nice if we can repeat this in STAN.

Many thanks again,
Baoyue Li

