Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

> Took me a while to understand what you said, but it makes sense to me now. I assume that iterations that fail after a single leapfrog step are reflected by treedepth = 0, or is it -1?

It’s the tree_depth =  -1 iterations that are indicative of an issue — although be careful because we changed
the definition of tree depth in 2.2.0 to be consistent with the typical computer science definition, in which
case it will be tree_depth = 0.

> The funky tail business can be explained by the cyclical pattern in the regression line. You could theoretically shift the regression line by one period and scale it by the reproduction factor so that it still fits the data. I tried to anchor the model to prevent this (by making sure that the log CDF would be close to -Inf for unlikely values), but apparently this now backfires with the mass matrix no longer being normalized. I guess another way to do it would be to truncate some of the distributions; there is reasonable prior knowledge of what the parameter values should(n't) be and maybe I just wasn't telling it very well to the model :).

Here’s my guess as to what is happening: the anchor introduces a region of very high curvature when the log CDF rapidly jumps to -infinty.  In order to
explore that region the integrator requires a very small step size or it will diverge and any samples rejected.  If you naively set the target acceptance
rate the step size will be too high and this region will essentially be ignored.  This will induce some bias in your samples, although the size of the bias
will depend on the specifics of your model.  This is one of the reasons why we increased the default target acceptance probability to 0.8.

Now this also manifests in adaptation.  When you enter this region the samples are continuously rejected until the step size becomes small enough
that the integrator is reasonably accurate.  These samples, however, bias the covariance estimates low which leads to the very small metric elements.

So try running with a larger target acceptance probability and see if that helps at all.

