Hi Dan,

Thank you for taking the time to look into my problem.  I'm trying to do a logistic regression, in which Y is the response, env1[i] is the true environmental value associated with site i. However, I don't get to observe env[i]. What I observe instead is env1table[i, ], which is a vector of possible covariate values associated with i, each having a probability determined by the vector pcatTable[i, ]. Since I don't know what's the actual environmental value associated with site i, I sample the env1 values in OpenBUGS using the statement:

...

for (i in 1:N) {

  env1[i] <- env1Table[i,k[i]]

  k[i]~dcat(pcatTable[i,])

...

In Stan, I would translate this by declaring k as an integer in the parameters, and then sampling env1Table[i, ] using categorical(pcatTable[i,]). However this isn't possible as integer parameters can't be declared in the parameters block. If k is declared as real in the parameters block, then I can't retrieve env1 using env1Table[i,k[i]]as the index k[i] can't be a real number (it has to be an integer). The  code I submitted in my last post, putting k in the data, was a desperate attempt to force k to be an integer.

About your last question, I was just trying to come up with the simplest possible model for Stan to test the code. Hence I only considered one variable in the Stan code vs. the three I had in OpenBugs.

Jorge

